00:03:26.728 --> 00:03:27.399
Makarand: Hello, everyone.

00:03:28.082 --> 00:03:28.932
I'm Makarand Mane.

00:03:29.565 --> 00:03:32.781
I have been building
WordPress websites since 2011.

00:03:33.428 --> 00:03:37.591
For more than a decade, I have
worked on websites, client projects,

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:40.082
and I run my own WordPress agency.

00:03:40.488 --> 00:03:47.241
But there is one thing I wish I
understood much earlier: accessibility.

00:03:49.658 --> 00:03:52.274
This is not a talk from
accessibility expert.

00:03:52.994 --> 00:03:57.793
This is a story about how I spent
these years building a website

00:03:58.156 --> 00:04:02.864
without really thinking about
accessibility, and how one person

00:04:03.426 --> 00:04:06.333
changed the way I look at the websites.

00:04:08.922 --> 00:04:13.730
So let me start with how I
used to define a good website.

00:04:15.037 --> 00:04:17.015
Before that, uh, just little about me.

00:04:17.461 --> 00:04:20.709
Uh, I'm building websites
with WordPress since 2011.

00:04:21.425 --> 00:04:25.072
Uh, I am self-taught, uh, full
stack developer, and I run an

00:04:25.081 --> 00:04:27.080
agency, WPGenius Solution LLP.

00:04:27.793 --> 00:04:31.272
Over the years, I have worked on
many different website projects,

00:04:32.653 --> 00:04:33.918
different, different technologies.

00:04:35.346 --> 00:04:41.538
And like most developers, I cared about
design, performance, SEO, responsiveness,

00:04:41.545 --> 00:04:47.479
and making client happy, but
accessibility was not part of my thinking.

00:04:48.928 --> 00:04:52.850
I didn't realize that there was
a gap the way I am building the

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:58.644
websites because this was basic
accidentally… Basically, my checklist

00:05:01.214 --> 00:05:04.471
When I thought about good
websites, I would ask

00:05:07.354 --> 00:05:08.539
Does it look good?

00:05:09.534 --> 00:05:11.279
Does it, it, it is responsive?

00:05:12.991 --> 00:05:14.991
Is it fast enough to load?

00:05:16.268 --> 00:05:17.587
It is SEO friendly

00:05:19.942 --> 00:05:22.002
Does it work in all modern browsers?

00:05:23.215 --> 00:05:26.199
And finally, is client
my- is my client happy?

00:05:26.781 --> 00:05:27.157
Yes?

00:05:31.678 --> 00:05:33.459
There is one important thing is missing:

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:37.233
Accessibility.

00:05:40.731 --> 00:05:43.038
Honestly, I didn't even
realize it was missing.

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:46.092
I did- didn't really think about it.

00:05:46.764 --> 00:05:48.255
I want to make one thing clear.

00:05:49.397 --> 00:05:52.909
It wasn't because I didn't care,
because I didn't understand it.

00:05:53.916 --> 00:05:55.399
I knew the word accessibility.

00:05:55.695 --> 00:05:58.848
I had seen accessibility scores,
screen readers exist, alt text

00:05:58.871 --> 00:06:02.249
exist, but I didn't understand
the people behind those things.

00:06:02.871 --> 00:06:05.468
And then one day, something
happened in my life.

00:06:12.729 --> 00:06:15.378
So at our office, we
regularly hire interns.

00:06:15.951 --> 00:06:19.144
The idea is to give students an
opportunity to work with real

00:06:19.161 --> 00:06:21.998
projects so they can understand
how the real world work.

00:06:22.835 --> 00:06:29.928
Uh, just two years before, uh, one
22-year, uh, old kid joined my office, and

00:06:29.992 --> 00:06:32.308
he found my agency through, uh, Google.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:37.999
He was really interested in working
with us, so I gave him a chance.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.657
He's interested in modern web
technologies, GSAP animations,

00:06:42.129 --> 00:06:43.449
and he's a really good coder.

00:06:44.942 --> 00:06:49.007
So, and while he was working with
us, I noticed something unusual

00:06:51.295 --> 00:06:55.031
I noticed the way he
was using his computer.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.635
He was sitting very close.

00:07:01.541 --> 00:07:03.115
He was wearing thick glasses.

00:07:04.323 --> 00:07:06.269
He was using a screen to zoom.

00:07:07.141 --> 00:07:10.322
Then he move face like
this, uh, near the screen.

00:07:11.685 --> 00:07:17.354
He's tilting his head to see the
text properly, and he's still coding.

00:07:18.513 --> 00:07:24.692
He was working with the browser, dev
tools, writing code, building website.

00:07:25.602 --> 00:07:29.750
He was using his, uh, MacBook's
track, trackpad to zoom the screen

00:07:32.094 --> 00:07:35.327
At first, looking at this, I was curious.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:40.631
I wanted to know why
he's working like this.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:51.061
So I ask him, "Why do you need to
look at the screen like that?" And

00:07:51.061 --> 00:07:53.594
that question changed everything.

00:07:57.305 --> 00:08:01.351
So when he, he told me the, his
story, why, what happened with him.

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:08.361
So from the beginning, he was a very well
kid, but one day he fell from the large,

00:08:08.666 --> 00:08:14.810
uh, pile of soil when he was a child,
and after that his eyesight was affected.

00:08:16.414 --> 00:08:17.255
He wasn't blind.

00:08:18.123 --> 00:08:19.409
He wasn't unable to work.

00:08:22.009 --> 00:08:25.619
He had simply learned a different
way to use the computer.

00:08:30.204 --> 00:08:32.638
He used the built-in
Zoom features of MacBook.

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:36.058
He would zoom the screen when he needed.

00:08:36.563 --> 00:08:40.448
Sometimes he would zoom the
browser, dev tools, everything.

00:08:41.058 --> 00:08:42.962
A- And there is no barriers.

00:08:43.349 --> 00:08:47.811
He found a way to work independently,
and the thing that really

00:08:47.813 --> 00:08:50.601
stayed with, with me, this was

00:08:53.305 --> 00:08:54.742
So he's a really good developer.

00:08:54.841 --> 00:08:58.047
His eyesight did not define
what he could accomplish.

00:09:01.982 --> 00:09:04.662
That was my first real
lesson in accessibility.

00:09:07.321 --> 00:09:11.060
Then I rememb- then I remembered
something I had seen for many years.

00:09:14.141 --> 00:09:18.802
These are the things, performance,
accessibility, best practice, SEO.

00:09:19.382 --> 00:09:24.121
Where we s-saw all these things
on the PageSpeed Insights page.

00:09:25.634 --> 00:09:31.992
So I really cared about the performance,
making the website SEO-friendly, uh,

00:09:32.268 --> 00:09:38.187
getting a good scores, but there was a
accessibility, but I didn't notice that.

00:09:39.545 --> 00:09:44.050
So now I started looking at that
accessibility part differently.

00:09:46.584 --> 00:09:51.236
Because now I had seen a real
person in front of me who helped

00:09:51.236 --> 00:09:52.981
me understand why it matter.

00:09:56.612 --> 00:09:59.991
I knew the word accessibility,
but I didn't know the people.

00:10:01.448 --> 00:10:03.745
There was a screen read-
screen readers exist.

00:10:04.683 --> 00:10:06.503
There are alt text exist.

00:10:07.861 --> 00:10:14.079
Accessibility existed as a concept,
but I never supposed that accessibility

00:10:14.163 --> 00:10:15.921
should be part of websites I am building.

00:10:17.892 --> 00:10:19.887
But I didn't understand why it mattered.

00:10:21.518 --> 00:10:26.123
I had never needed to zoom my entire
screen to read something, right?

00:10:28.088 --> 00:10:30.821
I had never experienced a
website from that perspective.

00:10:31.847 --> 00:10:34.804
So why accessibility idea to me?

00:10:35.874 --> 00:10:38.362
My intern turned that
idea into real person

00:10:41.081 --> 00:10:45.363
How many website did I build
like this so many years?

00:10:47.582 --> 00:10:48.992
I am working since 2011.

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:55.501
There are a thou- many website I shipped
without thinking about accessibility, and

00:10:55.501 --> 00:10:58.258
now I started wondering how many of them?

00:10:59.659 --> 00:11:00.622
Really, I don't know.

00:11:05.184 --> 00:11:07.073
Honestly, that question stayed with me.

00:11:08.622 --> 00:11:10.588
I wasn't intentionally excluding anyone.

00:11:11.596 --> 00:11:17.893
I wa- I simply wasn't thinking about
them because I didn't know the people

00:11:21.856 --> 00:11:26.390
I simply thinking, I, I, I s- I wasn't
simply, uh, thinking about that.

00:11:30.173 --> 00:11:34.093
I was thinking about the design, I was
thinking about the code, SEO performance.

00:11:35.005 --> 00:11:36.432
I'm ma- making my client happy.

00:11:37.734 --> 00:11:42.512
But I wasn't thinking, can
everyone actually use this website?

00:11:44.368 --> 00:11:45.173
That was the gap.

00:11:50.652 --> 00:11:54.979
So after, after this, I
started think noticing.

00:11:56.804 --> 00:12:00.533
I started paying attention to
what the tools were showing me.

00:12:08.252 --> 00:12:13.941
I started noticing the things I previously
ignored, like low color contrast,

00:12:14.533 --> 00:12:19.774
text that, that was difficult to read,
elements overlapping to each other,

00:12:20.669 --> 00:12:27.336
layout problems, missing or incorrect
semantics, heading structure, alt text.

00:12:30.933 --> 00:12:34.364
Some of, some of these things
were not completely new to me.

00:12:34.974 --> 00:12:37.879
For example, I already
know the alt text exists.

00:12:38.135 --> 00:12:40.473
Headings, we have different,
different kind of headings.

00:12:41.251 --> 00:12:45.842
But I mostly knew them from the
point of SEO and development.

00:12:48.612 --> 00:12:51.254
Now I understand another side of them.

00:12:52.166 --> 00:12:55.441
These things could affect
whether someone could actually

00:12:55.442 --> 00:12:56.951
understand and use the website.

00:13:00.004 --> 00:13:03.492
But I didn't suddenly
become an Excel expert.

00:13:05.458 --> 00:13:08.886
I, I don't know suddenly
e- everything about

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.239
Web, uh, coding accessibility guidelines.

00:13:14.227 --> 00:13:16.788
I just started asking
better questions to me.

00:13:22.469 --> 00:13:25.349
Like can someone, uh, read this?

00:13:25.876 --> 00:13:27.340
Can someone zoom properly?

00:13:27.819 --> 00:13:30.096
Can someone navigate without using mouse?

00:13:30.847 --> 00:13:33.102
Can someone understand the form properly?

00:13:34.046 --> 00:13:38.843
Can assistive technologies understand
what elements already used in the website?

00:13:40.688 --> 00:13:45.282
Those questions were enough to start
changing how I build the websites.

00:13:47.646 --> 00:13:53.976
So if I could go back to 2011,
what would I tell myself?

00:13:56.249 --> 00:13:59.842
What I wish I should knew before 15 years

00:14:02.492 --> 00:14:05.304
I wouldn't call myself
accessibility, uh, expert.

00:14:05.949 --> 00:14:08.885
I would tell myself to start
with few simple things.

00:14:10.521 --> 00:14:11.817
Let people see the content.

00:14:12.641 --> 00:14:14.344
Let people navigate without mouse.

00:14:15.762 --> 00:14:17.324
Use the right HTML semantics.

00:14:18.319 --> 00:14:22.142
Don't make people guess
what they can't see.

00:14:23.572 --> 00:14:25.406
And give the people
information they can't see.

00:14:26.107 --> 00:14:28.276
These are the five things
I wanted to talk about.

00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:39.444
First one, let the
people see your content.

00:14:43.168 --> 00:14:47.499
This means like a color contrast,
readable text, and zooming.

00:14:49.313 --> 00:14:52.995
We usually design a
website on our own screen.

00:14:54.983 --> 00:14:55.985
We know how it looks.

00:14:56.718 --> 00:15:02.055
We know where everything is, but
not everyone see it in the same way.

00:15:03.664 --> 00:15:04.958
Look at these two examples.

00:15:07.386 --> 00:15:12.090
So on the left, we have a poor
contrast, a gray button with gray text.

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:18.182
And on the right-hand side, we have better
contrast, blue button with white text.

00:15:20.047 --> 00:15:24.653
The content may be same, but the
second example is much easier to read.

00:15:26.684 --> 00:15:29.172
This is one of the simple
thing I started noticing.

00:15:31.031 --> 00:15:33.189
My intern was doing the, this every day.

00:15:34.136 --> 00:15:39.632
For him, zoom was not a just convenience,
it was a part of how he used his computer.

00:15:42.252 --> 00:15:44.975
This is something I wish
I had tested much earlier.

00:15:45.478 --> 00:15:48.144
A website may look perfect at 100%.

00:15:48.424 --> 00:15:49.382
Look at this example.

00:15:50.328 --> 00:15:53.290
The website is looking
properly when it is 100%.

00:15:54.031 --> 00:15:59.611
So if… But what happens
if we zoom it to 100%?

00:16:02.563 --> 00:16:03.327
Look at the GIF.

00:16:04.103 --> 00:16:06.835
A bad website with bad accessibility.

00:16:08.088 --> 00:16:14.097
What you are watching here, look
at how the layout changes when

00:16:14.097 --> 00:16:15.266
there is a bad accessibility.

00:16:15.897 --> 00:16:18.045
The elements are
overlapping on each other.

00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:21.400
There are icons going above the text.

00:16:23.465 --> 00:16:25.011
And the text is not readable properly.

00:16:25.997 --> 00:16:30.534
Few buttons are not clickable because
they messed up with other, uh, elements.

00:16:31.692 --> 00:16:35.222
Sometime, uh, there might be a
horizontal scrolling on your website.

00:16:37.564 --> 00:16:42.742
The text is cutting off button
become useless because it is

00:16:42.756 --> 00:16:44.120
overlapped with another content.

00:16:46.875 --> 00:16:50.796
So you can see the right
example of accessibility.

00:16:51.286 --> 00:16:54.552
So when you zoom, the content
should, uh, adjust itself.

00:16:58.943 --> 00:17:02.012
And ask, can the user still
reach the important content?

00:17:02.527 --> 00:17:04.304
This is the kind of
things I started noticing.

00:17:05.013 --> 00:17:09.184
And once you start testing this way,
you can see your website differently.

00:17:10.866 --> 00:17:14.730
Now let's look at another simple
test, keyboard navigation.

00:17:15.535 --> 00:17:17.203
And this is not actually new to me.

00:17:18.319 --> 00:17:20.677
Let me t- tell my story
of my college days.

00:17:21.423 --> 00:17:27.431
So at my college days, I used
to have a mouse with trackballs.

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:30.749
Have you used mouse with trackballs?

00:17:32.145 --> 00:17:35.941
And they used to collect a
lot of dust when you're using

00:17:35.943 --> 00:17:38.298
them, and that dust is, uh-

00:17:40.914 --> 00:17:43.614
making that, uh, mouse difficult to use.

00:17:44.975 --> 00:17:48.259
So most of my college, uh,
computers having that mouse,

00:17:48.267 --> 00:17:50.115
and we are not able to use that.

00:17:50.512 --> 00:17:55.052
So after that, I, uh, started to
use computer with only keyboard.

00:17:56.087 --> 00:18:00.762
I was using, uh, arrow keys, I was
using tabs, I was using Enter, shortcut

00:18:00.771 --> 00:18:05.644
keys, uh, on Windows, uh, Windows,
uh, Command key, and then Alt key.

00:18:06.144 --> 00:18:08.912
So like that way, I was
used to get my work done.

00:18:09.521 --> 00:18:13.651
At that time, I never thought
of this i- this is accessibility

00:18:15.806 --> 00:18:19.017
I was just using keyboard because,
uh, the mouse wasn't working properly.

00:18:20.183 --> 00:18:24.051
Someone else may use the keyboard because
the mouse isn't an option for them

00:18:26.138 --> 00:18:30.804
The same feature can be a convenience
for one person and necessity for another.

00:18:33.705 --> 00:18:35.586
Okay, look at this GIF.

00:18:38.145 --> 00:18:42.006
This, uh, this is a small
slideshow I have made for you.

00:18:42.655 --> 00:18:48.977
So here I am navigating my, uh, website
header using a keyboard, using tabs.

00:18:49.801 --> 00:18:54.203
And I am able to easily navigate to
important elements on my website.

00:18:54.471 --> 00:18:57.480
I was able to, uh, jump on each menu.

00:18:58.911 --> 00:19:03.358
And at the end I, I'm able to
click on, um, About using Enter

00:19:03.629 --> 00:19:05.439
and the About page is opening.

00:19:06.546 --> 00:19:11.122
Okay, so this is very simple
test you can do and see.

00:19:11.412 --> 00:19:15.387
I, uh… Using the keyboard you can
check, can I reach the important elements?

00:19:15.437 --> 00:19:17.787
Uh, can I see where the focus is?

00:19:17.817 --> 00:19:19.714
Uh, can I operate the controls?

00:19:20.153 --> 00:19:22.583
And can I move through the
page without getting stuck?

00:19:25.275 --> 00:19:28.714
Sometimes one of the simplest
accessibility tests is to put the mouse

00:19:28.761 --> 00:19:31.552
aside and try y- on your own website.

00:19:32.105 --> 00:19:36.278
So let's unplug your mouse and test
this on our, on your own websites.

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:44.947
Okay.

00:19:45.297 --> 00:19:47.117
The third thing is semantic HTML.

00:19:47.783 --> 00:19:50.372
It sounds technical, but
the idea is very simple.

00:19:51.081 --> 00:19:53.152
Use the right HTML element for the job.

00:19:53.652 --> 00:19:59.023
For example, we can make div
look exactly like a button.

00:20:02.104 --> 00:20:03.287
But it is still a div.

00:20:04.283 --> 00:20:06.173
The browser doesn't know this is a button.

00:20:07.093 --> 00:20:10.436
If you use the actual button element,
the browser already knows that.

00:20:11.434 --> 00:20:13.587
The browser understand
its meaning and behavior.

00:20:15.928 --> 00:20:20.094
So if we just use our div and
give the onClick eleme- onClick

00:20:20.129 --> 00:20:25.017
event, it will work natural, but
that's not the right way to use it.

00:20:25.716 --> 00:20:27.641
So for that, we have the button element.

00:20:30.046 --> 00:20:32.497
So don't just make something look right.

00:20:33.216 --> 00:20:34.263
Give it a right meaning.

00:20:37.301 --> 00:20:39.293
The browser already knows what is button.

00:20:39.735 --> 00:20:40.467
Let it help you.

00:20:44.607 --> 00:20:45.607
Next is a form.

00:20:46.464 --> 00:20:49.631
We build form all the times.

00:20:50.307 --> 00:20:51.346
Different, different forms.

00:20:51.682 --> 00:20:52.466
Contact form,

00:20:54.908 --> 00:20:57.603
registration forms, login
forms, checkout forms.

00:20:58.267 --> 00:20:58.931
A form

00:21:01.824 --> 00:21:05.560
A form can look beautiful but
still be difficult to use it.

00:21:06.492 --> 00:21:08.305
Look at this example on the side.

00:21:10.328 --> 00:21:15.582
Imagine I see the field called as email,
but the label isn't properly connected

00:21:17.619 --> 00:21:18.577
and I make a mistake.

00:21:19.597 --> 00:21:23.965
Then I get a message, "Something
went wrong. Invalid input.

00:21:24.682 --> 00:21:25.876
Please fix all these errors."

00:21:28.131 --> 00:21:31.219
That doesn't define what the form wants.

00:21:32.562 --> 00:21:36.635
And so tell the user what the
field is, tell them what went

00:21:36.639 --> 00:21:39.530
wrong, and tell them how to fix it.

00:21:40.898 --> 00:21:42.003
Look at this example.

00:21:42.810 --> 00:21:46.067
Here we have proper labels
all the time visible.

00:21:47.410 --> 00:21:51.606
When there, there is an incorrect
format of email, it is showing you have

00:21:51.606 --> 00:21:57.928
to, uh, enter the proper valid email
address, and there is example also given.

00:21:59.198 --> 00:22:03.866
If the input field is, uh, required,
then it is properly written.

00:22:03.896 --> 00:22:07.789
Please enter your comment
because that's required.

00:22:09.298 --> 00:22:12.852
So don't make users guess what
the website want from them.

00:22:18.074 --> 00:22:22.838
And the fifth thing is give the
information people can't see.

00:22:25.211 --> 00:22:26.801
This is where alt text comes in.

00:22:30.366 --> 00:22:32.302
I already knew about alt text.

00:22:32.991 --> 00:22:34.274
It is used for SEO.

00:22:35.427 --> 00:22:37.916
We have to give the, give
it, uh, on the image.

00:22:38.322 --> 00:22:39.620
That's a SEO requirement, right?

00:22:40.023 --> 00:22:42.178
But I didn't fully
understand what it meant for.

00:22:43.045 --> 00:22:46.532
Someone could see the
image, and someone couldn't.

00:22:48.226 --> 00:22:49.658
Look at this example on the slide.

00:22:50.509 --> 00:22:55.443
We have an image, a developer
is working in front of laptop.

00:22:56.805 --> 00:23:01.634
So if we write a proper alt text,
a developer working on a laptop.

00:23:02.763 --> 00:23:08.946
So someone, person who can't read,
see the image, and he, if he is using

00:23:08.946 --> 00:23:13.523
the screen reader, the screen reader
will announce a develop- an image

00:23:13.549 --> 00:23:15.445
of a developer working on a laptop.

00:23:19.868 --> 00:23:25.105
So the important point is don't use
file names as an alternative text.

00:23:26.222 --> 00:23:31.017
Instead, describe what the
image communicates to the user.

00:23:32.568 --> 00:23:34.831
In this example, a developer
working on a laptop.

00:23:36.761 --> 00:23:38.930
A sighted user can see the image.

00:23:39.783 --> 00:23:42.975
A screen reader user may hear
it, hear the alternative text.

00:23:44.509 --> 00:23:48.423
The important question is not only
what alt text will help Google.

00:23:48.997 --> 00:23:53.761
The better question is, what information
does the user need from this image?

00:23:57.051 --> 00:24:00.626
Now let's talk about the u-
tools I use as a starting point.

00:24:01.987 --> 00:24:04.109
First, automated checks.

00:24:05.248 --> 00:24:07.924
The Lighthouse and PageSpeed
Insights that we all know.

00:24:09.629 --> 00:24:13.424
They are very useful because they
help us find potential problems.

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:15.486
But I don't want to stop here.

00:24:16.282 --> 00:24:17.664
Let's go with some manual checks.

00:24:20.221 --> 00:24:21.398
Try to zoom your website.

00:24:22.004 --> 00:24:25.996
See if every- everything is going
good or something is broken.

00:24:27.109 --> 00:24:29.971
Throw away your mouse
and use your keyboard

00:24:32.122 --> 00:24:37.016
use, uh, DevTools in your
browser to check your elements.

00:24:39.126 --> 00:24:42.676
And finally, and wherever
appropriate, prior screen reader.

00:24:45.881 --> 00:24:48.361
Automated tools can find some problems.

00:24:49.238 --> 00:24:52.671
They can't tell you whether
the website is actually usable.

00:24:54.052 --> 00:24:56.812
So you have to also, uh,
rely on the manual checks.

00:24:57.264 --> 00:25:01.004
You have to see if, uh, your
website is really usable.

00:25:07.837 --> 00:25:12.436
And this is probably the biggest
change I want to make in my workflow.

00:25:15.257 --> 00:25:21.756
Accessibility should not be the something
we remember five minutes before launch.

00:25:25.790 --> 00:25:27.964
It starts with design.

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:30.495
Think about it during design.

00:25:31.084 --> 00:25:32.679
Build with accessibility in mind.

00:25:33.371 --> 00:25:35.928
Test your code, fix it, and repeat.

00:25:39.551 --> 00:25:44.956
We don't need to make everything perfect
on day one, but accessibility should be

00:25:44.956 --> 00:25:47.250
part of our normal development workflow.

00:25:53.633 --> 00:25:58.068
This is the main idea I want
to leave you with today.

00:25:59.505 --> 00:26:02.546
Accessibility isn't about making
websites for other people.

00:26:06.107 --> 00:26:10.145
It's about making sure the
website we build don't create

00:26:10.178 --> 00:26:11.923
unnecessary barriers for anyone.

00:26:15.189 --> 00:26:18.318
Someone may have permanent disability,
someone may have partially.

00:26:19.035 --> 00:26:20.635
Someone may have temporary injury.

00:26:22.130 --> 00:26:24.238
Someone may simply need a larger text.

00:26:25.495 --> 00:26:28.164
Someone is using only keyboard.

00:26:28.924 --> 00:26:30.294
Someone might be using screen reader.

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:32.797
Someone may need to zoom the screen.

00:26:33.784 --> 00:26:39.861
Most of the time, we don't know who
is going to use our website, right?

00:26:40.835 --> 00:26:44.493
So let's not build only
for the person we imagine.

00:26:45.093 --> 00:26:46.484
Let's build for real people.

00:26:51.294 --> 00:26:52.454
And that's my story.

00:26:53.089 --> 00:26:58.182
I spent more than 10 years building
websites before I really understand

00:26:58.182 --> 00:26:59.671
the importance of the accessibility.

00:27:00.362 --> 00:27:05.126
It took one intern showing me
how he used a computer to change

00:27:05.127 --> 00:27:07.071
the way I looked at websites.

00:27:09.316 --> 00:27:14.851
I'm still learning accessibility, and I'm
not an expert, and I think that's okay.

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:18.578
You don't have to become an
accessibility expert overnight.

00:27:19.569 --> 00:27:20.534
Start with one thing.

00:27:21.152 --> 00:27:22.185
Check your contrast.

00:27:22.644 --> 00:27:24.307
Try to zoom 200%.

00:27:25.521 --> 00:27:27.636
Put your mouse aside, use your keyboard.

00:27:28.559 --> 00:27:32.321
Check your forms, look at the
headings, and try a screen reader.

00:27:34.096 --> 00:27:36.119
Then improve one thing at a time

00:27:38.905 --> 00:27:45.978
I wish I had learned this in 2011,
but you don't have to wait that long.

00:27:46.888 --> 00:27:47.415
